WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — interface-economics (180s) Narrator Edwin

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:07.659
First, single handoff failures carry exact price tags, like losing a Mars mission over mismatched units.

2
00:00:08.219 --> 00:00:16.088
Second, giant national software-failure numbers come from broad surveys and models, never counting handoffs alone.

3
00:00:16.648 --> 00:00:23.610
Third, checking what each side assumes is cheap, but one team pays while everyone else benefits.

4
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:31.885
An interface is any handoff where one group's output becomes another group's input—from theorists who specify behavior

5
00:00:31.885 --> 00:00:38.749
to builders who write code, or between two levels of a software and hardware stack. Catastrophic failures

6
00:00:38.749 --> 00:00:47.640
routinely strike not because one module miscalculated internally, but because both sides trusted an unwritten assumption nobody checked.

7
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:55.668
The space agency's Mars Surveyor 1998 project spent three hundred twenty-seven point six million dollars before

8
00:00:55.668 --> 00:01:03.607
thruster impulses sent in pound-force seconds were read as metric newton-seconds. Three years earlier, Ariane 5 Flight

9
00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:11.949
501 lost four Cluster satellites when reused guidance software exceeded an unchecked horizontal velocity envelope on launch.

10
00:01:12.509 --> 00:01:19.576
Our study enforces one strict accounting rule: every published figure carries its exact evidence kind and is

11
00:01:19.576 --> 00:01:28.606
never added to incompatible estimates. Audited single-incident losses sit beside national economic models—like fifteen point eight billion

12
00:01:28.606 --> 00:01:37.113
dollars a year for construction interoperability and two point four one trillion for poor software quality—without synthetic sums.

13
00:01:37.673 --> 00:01:44.415
When an unexamined mismatch slips past a boundary, it travels upward looking like well-formed input to

14
00:01:44.415 --> 00:01:52.280
every layer above it, charging technical-debt interest on every later change. Formally, an explicit boundary check pays

15
00:01:52.280 --> 00:02:00.410
off whenever its tiny per-crossing cost stays below violation probability times summed losses across all downstream levels.

16
00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:09.216
Our three-dimensional funnel visualizes cross-team payloads passing through a machine-checked boundary gate that verifies physical

17
00:02:09.216 --> 00:02:17.336
units, numeric bounds, and operating envelopes before commit. In machine-learning pipelines and automated code generation alike,

18
00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:24.314
most hidden technical debt sits in glue code, configuration, and undeclared consumers right outside the model.

19
00:02:24.874 --> 00:02:33.472
Decades of compatibility economics prove why rational teams under-provide interface specs: writing and checking boundary invariants

20
00:02:33.472 --> 00:02:39.510
is a private cost today that produces a shared public good for everyone else. Automated code

21
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:47.977
generators cross these exact same seams, so generating code faster without checking boundaries simply compounds integration debt.

22
00:02:48.537 --> 00:02:55.942
Because no primary source isolates a single national dollar figure for interface failures alone, this essay

23
00:02:55.942 --> 00:03:04.316
refuses to invent one. Instead, Section 7 defines three checkable protocols—blind mishap coding with bootstrap intervals,

24
00:03:04.316 --> 00:03:14.212
seeded fault injection measured in both joules and dollars, and prose-versus-machine-checked handoff trials—to test every proposition directly.
